        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 21,
          "name": "Prospective mapping of viral mutations that escape antibodies used to treat COVID-19",
          "item": {
            "@type": "Book",
            "name": "Prospective mapping of viral mutations that escape antibodies used to treat COVID-19",
            "url": "https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.abf9302",
            "identifier": {
              "@type": "PropertyValue",
              "propertyID": "DOI",
              "value": "10.1126/science.abf9302"
            },
            "author": [
              {
                "@type": "Person",
                "name": "Tyler N. Starr"
              },
              {
                "@type": "Person",
                "name": "Allison J. Greaney"
              },
              {
                "@type": "Person",
                "name": "Amin Addetia"
              },
              {
                "@type": "Person",
                "name": "William W. Hannon"
              },
              {
                "@type": "Person",
                "name": "Manish C. Choudhary"
              },
              {
                "@type": "Person",
                "name": "Adam S. Dingens"
              },
              {
                "@type": "Person",
                "name": "Jonathan Z. Li"
              },
              {
                "@type": "Person",
                "name": "Jesse D. Bloom"
              }
            ],
            "datePublished": "2021",
            "publisher": {
              "@type": "Organization",
              "name": "American Association for the Advancement of Science (AAAS)"
            },
            "description": "<jats:title>Mapping antibody escape in SARS-CoV-2</jats:title>\n          <jats:p>\n            Several antibodies are in use or under development as therapies to treat COVID-19. As new severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) variants emerge, it is important to predict whether they will remain susceptible to antibody treatment. Starr\n            <jats:italic>et al.</jats:italic>\n            used a yeast library that covers all mutations to the SARS-CoV-2 receptor-binding domain that do not strongly disrupt binding to the host receptor (ACE2) and mapped how these mutations affect binding to three leading anti–SARS-CoV-2 antibodies. The maps identify mutations that escape antibody binding, including a single mutation that escapes both antibodies in the Regeneron antibody cocktail. Many of the mutations that escape single antibodies are circulating in the human population.\n          </jats:p>\n          <jats:p>\n            <jats:italic>Science</jats:italic>\n            , this issue p.\n            <jats:related-article xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" ext-link-type=\"doi\" issue=\"6531\" page=\"850\" related-article-type=\"in-this-issue\" vol=\"371\" xlink:href=\"10.1126/science.abf9302\">850</jats:related-article>\n          </jats:p>",
            "volumeNumber": "371",
            "issueNumber": "6531",
            "pagination": "850-854"
          }
        },
        {
